  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/8/8f/Tales_of_Suspense_Vol_1_43.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "After a wind tunnel goes haywire in Tony's private lab, becoming so strong at one point as to actually topple a missile right off its mountings, it explodes with a [SOUNDEFFECT: text=BRAMMMMMM!] into some nearby mountains.&nbsp; Tony Stark has to rush over to the mishap and settle things down as Iron Man.\n\nBut after Stark arrives soon after to survey the damage, people begin disappearing into thin air!&nbsp; And a mysterious bubble surrounds Stark (who left his Iron Man armor behind in order to show up on the scene in person) and then begins sinking into the Earth!&nbsp; He descends right through the rock of the Earth into a \"strange underground world.\"\n\nKala, \"Queen of the Netherworld,\" welcomes Stark to her underground empire -- the people who disappeared from the lab are here too, being held hostage.&nbsp; She was after Stark all along but missed the target the first two times.&nbsp; No matter though - she now has hostages!\n\nTurns out these people are descendants of the people of Atlantis, while it was still above the surface of the waters, and who escaped the tidal wave that supposedly destroyed it by sinking down into the ocean in a glass dome, but it kept sinking every year until it \"reached the core of the globe, where it exists now as the <i>Netherworld</i>!\"\n\nWhen Stark asks what that has to do with him, Kala goes on to explain that \"the time is now to <i>invade the surface world</i>, which we alone once inhabited!\"&nbsp; She orders her general, Baxu, to show Stark around at their military weapons.&nbsp; Though their weaponry is far more advanced that anything that exists on the surface, they have no means with which to <i>transport</i> it, which is where Stark comes in.&nbsp; They've learned through their monitoring of surface television that Stark is \"America's greatest inventive genius,\" and since they don't have enough power to reach the surface, even <i>with </i>the incredible military might they show him, they need Stark to provide devices to blast a path upwards -- \"transistor-powered machines such as you alone can create!\"&nbsp; So the ultimatum is that he either invents a method of transporting their invasion forces and weapons to the surface, or his friends will be executed!\n\nWhen Stark points out that Iron Man <i>alone</i> could stop them, she reiterates her threat, adding that she'll also activate a machine that will actually reverse the Earth's axis, \"causing upheaval to the surface world, destroying all life there!\"\n\nStark then pretends to agree, even going so far as to make loud statements that he doesn't care about mankind's fate so long as his own skin is safe.&nbsp; Naturally, he demands a private lab and absolute privacy, and overnight, he fabricates another Iron Man suit, which he puts on to fight back and overwhelms Baxu.&nbsp; Iron Man reveals that his boss (Stark) actually liked him and suggests that <i>Baxu</i> should be in charge in the Netherworld instead of Kala.\n\nUpon learning that Iron Man has apparently traded places with Stark, Kala orders various weapons aimed at the intruder, but Iron Man thwarts them all.&nbsp; Upon surviving their every attack, it's time for Iron Man to go on the offensive.\n\nHe then grabs Kala and begins burrowing his way back to the surface with her (using \"nuclear-powered clippers\").&nbsp; As soon as she gets to the surface, the different atmosphere causes some \"hideous changes in you!\"&nbsp; She's turned into \"an old hag!\"&nbsp; Iron Man points out that this aging effect will apply to all her troops as well.&nbsp; She begs him to return her to the netherworld.&nbsp; She frees her hostages, and at the suggestion of Iron Man, turns toward her lieutenant Baxu, with the plan for them to rule the Netherworld wisely together, and thereby to abandon her plan to invade the surface world.\n\nOn the last panel, Tony Stark is back home at a cocktail party.&nbsp; When someone points out that his adventure under the Earth (how did they even <i>know</i> about it?) kept him away from the <i>girls</i> for a while, Tony only thinks \"That's what <i>you</i> think, bub!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "A phoned-in dud that drains your life force one insipid panel at a time.\nOne brief moment of unintended comedy prevents a full-scale meltdown.",
  "subtitle": "Silver Age on Autopilot -- to Heck With the Results",
  "review_text": "It's rare that we get a story like this that so quickly asks readers to check their intelligence at the door.&nbsp; It's the kind of warning label that should've been printed right on the splash page.\n\nOnly two issues after the horrible [LINK:1963:TOS_41_iron0|TOS #41], Stan and company follow it up with this fresh disaster, and my knee-jerk reaction is that it wasn't just one more droll story!&nbsp; No, this was about as close to \"phoning it in\" as you can get.\n\nA Netherworld civilization existing at the core of the Earth that originally descended in a bubble after the tidal wave hit that destroyed Atlantis?&nbsp; I'm guessing that it destroyed only a <i>portion</i> of it, since we've already seen the Sub-Mariner \"looking\" for Atlantis over in the pages of <i>Fantastic Four</i>.&nbsp; (If not, it seems like he needs to dive deeper, because apparently Atlantis now comes in <i>multiple flavors</i>!)\n\n[ASSET: right | ReverseEarthsAxis | width=8%] Regardless, whatever this <i>other</i> part of the Atlantis remnant is, it now exists deep in the core of the Earth, apparently with technology enough to actually reverse the axis of the Earth.\n[BR]\n[BR] But...they need Stark (naturally) to help transport their weaponry to the surface.\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | FlattenYouToAPancake | width=14%] Tony has to re-create his armor, which he does overnight (see page 9) and then, using dialogue that can only have been written by someone under 10 years old, indicates to Baxu that,\n[BR]\n[BR] \"I have only to stamp my foot to flatten you into a pancake.\"\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=What?]\n\nPages 10 and 11 simply show Iron Man warding off one attack after another in a panel-consuming, yet mind-numbingly dull sequence.\n\nWhat struck me as odd here was when Kala ordered their disintegrator cannon fired upon Iron Man (page 10).&nbsp; It's downright bizarre that Iron Man never once thought anything of it, especially as we just saw Tony himself inventing a similar device last month.&nbsp; But he takes no note of this or thinks about it in any way.\n\n[ASSET: right | NiceAndTidy | width=19%] Everything is wrapped up nicely in 13 pages, and Princess Kala and General Baxu seem to be headed for marriage and joint control over a now peaceful Netherworld.&nbsp; Tony returns to his old life (and his many girlfriends), and we're all poorer for it - [SPAN: severity=error; text=twelve cents and 13 pages we'll <i>never</i> get back].\n\nThe splash page credits indicate that the art was by Jack Kirby and inked by Don Heck, but it sure looked far more like Heck's work than Kirby's, so it must have been layouts or light penciling.&nbsp; But there was also that antenna on Iron Man's left shoulder.&nbsp; It's very inconsistently drawn and seems to be present whenever Kirby is involved with the artwork (see the aforementioned [LINK:1963:TOS_41_iron0|TOS #41] again).\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThis bubble city in the center of the Earth is beyond ridiculous and shows lazy plotting of the highest order.&nbsp; Will we ever be hearing from this Netherworld again, or are we now supposed to conveniently just forget that they exist?\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; text=I'm sorry, Stan, but their existence alone costs a rating point!&nbsp; <i>Ugh</i>!]\n\nAnd the planned invasion of the surface world?&nbsp; Oh my!&nbsp; Another in a long list of would-be invaders.&nbsp; I've repeatedly pointed out how long a list this is getting to be.&nbsp; People from below, from other dimensions, from outer space, even as far away as other <i>galaxies</i>, are falling over themselves to invade the Earth!&nbsp; What exactly does the surface of the Earth offer that's worth all this trouble?&nbsp; And how is it that they've all just <i>happened</i> to time their moment of conquest to the early 1960s?\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=Am I missing something here?]\n\n[ASSET: right  | OldHag | width=9%] I will say that I did at least laugh out loud when Kala turned into an \"old hag\" on the final page.&nbsp; It was hilarious because it was so unexpected!&nbsp; <b><i>Hahahaha!</i></b>\n[BR]\n[BR] Thanks, Stan, for at least one brief moment of enjoyment in an otherwise tedious tale.&nbsp; For that, I'll award an extra half point so that at least this one escapes bottom rung status.\n\n(And it more than compensates for the eye-popping moment of disbelief just before that -- that of seeing Iron Man return to the surface using his \"nuclear-powered clippers\" to bore a shaft upwards.)\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold-italic; text=What?]\n\nThere's no escaping it -- outside of the one moment of surprising hilarity, this book is becoming less <i>Tales of Suspense</i> and more <b><i>Tales of Offense</i></b> -- perhaps a new nickname to go alongside <i>Tales to Abolish</i>!&nbsp; At this rate, the title is doing all the heavy lifting.\n\n[DIVIDER]\n<b>Grade Justification:</b>\n\nThis issue isn't a meltdown like [LINK:1963:TOS_41_iron0|TOS #41], but it's still a slog.&nbsp; The plotting is lazy, the premise collapses under its own weight, and the resolution feels like someone was racing to catch a bus.&nbsp; Outside of one accidental laugh on the final page, there's nothing here that rises above \"why did I read this?\" territory.&nbsp; It drains your energy without earning it, landing squarely in the FR/GD zone.",
  "decoder_ring": "On the final panel Tony Stark thinks, \"That's what <i>you</i> think, bub!\"&nbsp; It's the first time I remember reading anyone being addressed as \"bub\" until now, and of course predates Wolverine's nearly constant use of the term by well over a decade.",
  "highlight": "One laugh, thirteen pages of Iron Man wading through Netherworld regret."
}